FMM stage 1: 2D Greengard-Rokhlin fast multipole method, numpy, CPU

Purpose: velocity of vortex particles (Biot-Savart) in O(N), as the
reference for the CUDA/MPI versions that follow.

Files
  direct.py   O(N^2) direct sum. u - i v = sum_j q_j K(r)/(z - z_j),
              q_j = Gamma_j/(2 pi i); stream function psi = Im sum q_j log(z - z_j)
              (point vortex psi only). Cores: point, gauss
              (K = 1 - exp(-r^2/delta^2)), algebraic (K = r^2/(r^2+delta^2)).
  fmm2.py     complex-log expansions with p terms: P2M, M2M, M2L, L2L, L2P,
              P2L, M2P (multipole evaluation), P2P.
              uniform(): full quadtree with L levels, M2L over the 27-box
              interaction lists.
              adaptive(): Morton-key quadtree, leaves hold <= s particles,
              Carrier-Greengard-Rokhlin U, V, W, X lists.
              The far field uses point vortices; the core is used in P2P only.
  test2.py    all tests; writes out/*.txt (%.17g) and ~/dns/img/fmm/*.png.

Run
  python3 test2.py              (about 10 minutes on the Mac; log in out/test2.log)
  python3 fmm2.py -n 100000 -p 16
  python3 direct.py -n 1000

Verified (tables in out/)
  op_exact.txt   M2M and L2L are exact identities: 2.3e-16 and 2.2e-16 at
                 p = 4..30.
  op_p2m.txt     multipole evaluation, box radius rho = sqrt(1/2), targets at
  op_m2l.txt     |z| = 2 and 3. M2L for offsets (2,0), (2,1), (2,2), (3,0).
  op_p2l.txt     P2L. Errors fall geometrically, below
                 (rho/(D-rho))^(p+1) except at p = 2, where the bound's
                 constant prefactor (omitted here) matters.
  err_vs_p.txt   full FMM, N = 20000, all points against the direct sum, max
                 relative error of the velocity:
                   p      4       8       12      16      20      24      30
                   unif   3.2e-4  7.5e-6  2.0e-7  7.6e-9  3.7e-10 1.3e-11 7.7e-14
                 The same for the adaptive tree on uniform, two thin sheets,
                 and a 1e-4 Gaussian blob plus background (tree depth 17).
                 The blob is about 3x worse at equal p.
                 Fitted rate 0.436 per order; worst-case bound
                 rho/(2-rho) = 0.547.
  highp.log      accuracy floor 1.2e-15 at p >= 38. Unscaled coefficients
                 show no overflow or round-off growth up to p = 50 and depth 17.
  p_needed.txt   p for max relative velocity error 1e-6 / 1e-10 / 1e-14:
                 12 / 22-24 / 34.
  core.txt       Gaussian blobs, uniform tree with leaf size h = 1/16, p = 30.
                 The far-field point-vortex error tracks exp(-(h/delta)^2)
                 (about 30x below it):
                   delta/h  0.2      0.25    0.3     0.4     0.5
                   err      2e-12    2.7e-9  4.0e-7  7.7e-5  1.0e-3
                 Rule: delta <= 0.2 h_leaf, or add the core correction for
                 well-separated pairs.
  time_vs_N.txt  p = 16, one Mac core, numpy:
                   N        1e4     1e5    1e6
                   uniform  0.14 s  0.85 s 7.7 s
                   adaptive 0.19 s  1.2 s  19.5 s (7.4 s of it is the Python tree build)
                   direct   1.6 s   153 s  6e4 s (estimated from 1000 targets)
                 log-log slopes for N >= 1e4: uniform 0.87, adaptive 1.02,
                 direct 2.28. The FMM is faster than the direct sum from
                 N of about 2000.
                 Error does not grow with N (1e-8 to 7e-8 at p = 16).
  ops_per_level.txt  N = 1e6, s = 40: about 27 M2L per box at the deep levels,
                 1.74e6 M2L at the leaf level, 1.4e8 P2P pair interactions.
  leafsize.txt   N = 3e5, p = 16. Without the tree build, the fastest leaf
                 size is s = 20 (P2P 1.2 s + M2L etc. 0.8 s).
                 P2P dominates the run time at s >= 40.

Notes
  psi from the FMM is the point-vortex stream function. The log terms carry
  no branch error because Gamma is real: Im(q log z) = -Gamma ln|z|/(2 pi).
  The tree is non-periodic, over the bounding square of the particles.

FMM stage 2: the same 2D FMM in CUDA on one GPU (fmm2.cu)

Files
  fmm2.cu    one CUDA program in the box.cu style. Input and output are raw
             doubles, N x (x, y, Gamma) in and N x (u, v, psi) out.
             Options: -p terms, -s max per leaf, -D max depth (20),
             -d Gaussian core, -t timings, -c m (direct-sum check of m
             targets on the GPU).
             - Morton keys and a thrust stable radix sort on the GPU.
             - Level-by-level tree build: binary search for counts, a scan
               for the children.
             - U/V/W/X lists in two passes (count, scan, fill), one thread
               per box.
             - P2M, M2M, M2L, P2L, L2L: one thread per (box, coefficient),
               deterministic, no atomics. Translation matrices per level and
               offset (49 for M2L) are built on the host.
             - L2P and M2P: one thread per particle.
             - P2P: one block of NW = 64 threads per leaf, with the source
               leaves staged through shared memory.
  gen2.c     particle generator (uniform, sheets, blob) for large N on
             FASRC, which has no numpy.
  gate2.py   make: inputs plus fmm2.py references. cmp: compares the GPU,
             fmm2.py and the direct sum.
  Makefile   make fmm2 gen2 (sm_80 + sm_90). FASRC:
             module load cuda/12.9.1-fasrc01.

Gate: same tree, same answer (out/gate2_tree.txt, out/gate2_cmp.txt)
  The tree and the lists are identical to fmm2.py: box count, depth, U, V,
  W and X counts in all 6 cases.
  GPU against fmm2.py, max relative difference of the velocity:
    uniform N=2e4 p=16       7.9e-16
    uniform N=2e4 p=24       5.5e-16
    sheets  N=2e4 p=16       3.6e-16
    uniform N=2e5 p=16       8.8e-16
    blob (tree depth 17) p=16 6.0e-14, p=24 1.8e-13
  Blob cases are at the problem's own round-off level. Moving every input
  coordinate by 1 ulp changes the exact direct sum by 1.1e-12 to 2.3e-12.
  With -fmad=false the GPU-Python difference drops 2-5x.
  Bug found and fixed: building the M2L table as 1/z^k overflowed |z^k|^2
  at depth 17 (NaN). It now uses powers of 1/z.

Time against N (out/stage2_tune.txt, out/scale1.log)
  One A100-SXM4-80GB, uniform particles, s = 64 (s = 32 for N <= 1e6),
  NW = 64. GPU compute time excludes reading the file and the copies to
  and from the host.
    N      p=16     p=24     error p=24 (direct check)
    1e4    0.007 s  0.016 s  1.5e-10
    1e5    0.016 s  0.022 s  5.5e-11
    1e6    0.020 s  0.038 s  8.3e-11
    1e7    0.13 s   0.154 s  8.0e-11
    1e8    0.97 s   1.11 s   7.7e-11 (p=16: 2.9e-9)
  About 11 ns per particle at 1e8 and p = 24. Below about 1e6 the time is
  dominated by fixed overheads: the host translation tables and launches.
  At 1e8 and p = 24: P2P 0.51 s, M2L 0.35 s (1.5e8 V pairs), sort 0.05 s,
  tree and lists 0.06 s, M2M and P2M 0.09 s. The copy to the host
  (2.4 GB) adds 0.86 s.
  M2L runs at about 1.1 TFLOP/s, 11% of the FP64 peak; this is the next
  thing to optimise.

Tuning (N = 1e7, p = 24)
  leaf size s:  16 -> 0.206 s, 32 -> 0.224, 64 -> 0.154, 128 -> 0.161,
                256 -> 0.32. Default: s = 64.
  P2P block NW: 32 and 64 are equal; 128 is 30% slower at 1e8. Default: 64.
  Clustered input, N = 1e7, p = 24:
    blob (depth 20): 0.19 s, error 1.2e-11 to 1.6e-10
    sheets:          0.21 s, error 4.3e-11
  The blob needs p = 26 to be safely under 1e-10, as in stage 1, where
  blobs ran about 3x worse at equal p.

FMM stage 3a: 3D Laplace and Biot-Savart, spherical harmonics, rotation-based translations

Files
  harm3.py   Dehnen (2014) solid harmonics, by recurrence:
               regular   Ups_n^m = (-1)^m r^n P_n^m e^{im phi} / (n+m)!
               irregular Thet_n^m = (-1)^m (n-m)! P_n^m e^{im phi} / r^(n+1)
             1/|r-x| = sum Ups_n^m(x) conj(Thet_n^m(r)).
             Rotation matrices: Wigner d = exp(beta A) with A = -i J_y,
             mapped into this basis as G = c^-1 d^T c, c = sqrt((n-m)!(n+m)!).
             The convention was measured by least squares, then checked to
             1e-14 up to n = 40.
  direct3.py direct Laplace potential and gradient, and the Biot-Savart
             velocity u = sum alpha x (x - x_j) / (4 pi r^3), with an optional
             Gaussian core.
  fmm3.py    numpy reference:
             - P2M, P2L, M2P/L2P (potential and gradient);
             - M2M/L2L/M2L, both direct O(p^4) and rotation-based O(p^3)
               (rotate to z, axial shift, rotate back);
             - adaptive octree with U/V/W/X lists;
             - Biot-Savart as 3 Laplace FMMs with an analytic curl.
             M2L uses the full truncation of Cheng-Greengard-Rokhlin (CGR99):
             multipole and local degree <= p, Thet up to degree 2p.
  fmm3.cu    CUDA, one file:
             - Morton octree and two-pass lists on the GPU (the stage-2 code
               in 3D);
             - M2M/M2L/L2L: one block per box, shared-memory rotate-shift-
               rotate; rotation tables per offset built on the host (343 M2L
               directions, 8+8 for M2M/L2L);
             - P2M/P2L/L2P/M2P use per-m column recurrences, so no atomics;
             - P2P: one block per leaf.
             -b runs Biot-Savart; -c m checks the first m particles against a
             direct sum on the GPU (eq. 57 of CGR99 plus max error).
  gen3.c     particles: cube, sphere (random polar angles, so concentrated at
             the poles), cylinder (r 0.05, h 1), sheets, blob; charges
             U(-0.5,0.5) or Biot alpha ~ N(0,1).
  gate3.py   CUDA against fmm3.py.
  sweep3.sh  error against p, and the CGR99 runs.
  test3.py   numpy operator gates.

Operator gates, numpy (out/3_op_exact.txt, 3_op_bounds.txt, 3_mirror.txt)
  M2M (direct and rotated) against P2M about the parent: 2.5e-16 to 3.6e-16,
    p = 4..30.
  L2L (rotated) keeps potential and gradient to 1e-15 to 4e-15.
  M2L rotated against direct O(p^4): 6e-15 (p=4), 9e-12 (p=16; the high
    coefficients are of size (n+k)!/rho^(n+k+1)).
  Truncation is below the bounds (5.17) P2M, (5.24) M2M and (5.27) M2L for
    every p and every offset (2,0,0), (2,1,0), (2,2,2), (3,0,0), with sources
    and targets at the cube corners.
  The worst single M2L, offset (2,0,0) with corner sources and corner
    targets, decays only about 0.69 per order (close to the 3/4 of the
    bound). The other offsets decay much faster.
  Mirror rules hold exactly (0.0):
    multipole and local, z -> -z: (-1)^(n+m) c
    x -> -x: (-1)^m conj(c)
    y -> -y: conj(c)
  The symmetry option is designed but off.

Gate: CUDA = numpy (gate3.py, hal A100)
  Same tree and identical U/V/W/X counts in all 6 cases.
  Max relative difference: 3e-16 to 4e-15 (cube, sheets, Biot-Savart);
  3e-14 for the blob potential (the conditioning level, as in stage 2).

Error against p in 3D (out/3_err_vs_p.txt, 3_rate.txt, img/fmm/err3_vs_p.png)
  N = 20000, s = 40, all points against the GPU direct sum.
  Fitted rate per order, p = 8..24:
    potential rel L2   0.51-0.52
    velocity max rel   0.56-0.62
  So the decay is close to the multipole-only (sqrt3/3)^p = 0.577^p, not the
  worst-case (3/4)^p.
  p = 30 (cube): potential L2 5.5e-11, velocity max 8.1e-11.
  p needed for 1e-10:
                 potential L2   velocity max
    cube         30             30
    blob         28             30
    sheets       28             20
  Gradient and potential max errors decay more slowly at high p (cube
  gradient max 4.6e-10 at p = 30).

CGR99 reproduction (out/3_cgr.txt)
  Their tables I-IX: cube, sphere, cylinder, with their p/s pairs 10/40,
  19/100, 29/180, N = 2e4..1e6, error eq. 57 on the first 100 particles.
  The "complicated object" tables X-XII are not reproducible (no geometry).
  Tree: levels agree in 40 of 42 runs; nonempty box counts agree within a few
    percent. The two differences are sphere 5e5 p19 (11 vs 10) and cylinder
    2e4 p19 (6 vs 5): different random samples.
  Error at the same p:
    p = 10  ours 6e-7 to 7e-6   against their 2.7e-4 to 8.4e-4  (100-1000x
                                                                 smaller)
    p = 19  ours 1e-9 to 4e-8   against their 2.3e-7 to 7e-7    (10-100x)
    p = 29  ours 3.6e-12 to 1.8e-10 against their 2.8e-11 to 2.8e-10 (ratio
            0.02 to 3, median about 0.15)
  Why: CGR99 pick an exponential (plane-wave) quadrature for 3, 6 or 9 digits.
  At 3 and 6 digits that quadrature, not p, sets their error (and two of
  their 6-digit tables are in single precision). Our M2L is exact up to the
  multipole truncation. At 9 digits both schemes are limited by the same
  p = 29 truncation and agree.
  Work: 65-161 M2L per nonempty box (189 maximum for a uniform interior),
  170-2900 P2P pairs per particle.
  GPU time (A100, without file I/O), p = 29, N = 5e5: about 1.2 s compute,
  of which M2L is about 1.0 s. The host rotation tables add 4.2 s (computed
  once; to be cached or moved to the GPU).

FMM stage 3, step A (M2L speed) and step B (periodic box); normalised arithmetic
  fmm3.cu now needs -T <tables from tables.py, mpmath 50 digits>.
  Coefficients are normalised and scaled per box, so every stored number is
  O(1) and nothing is a factorial at run time (DERIV.md sections 5-6).
  M2L modes:
    FMM_M2L=1  rotation, half spectrum (default)
    FMM_M2L=2  dense cuBLAS ZGEMM per offset; faster for p >= 25.
  Periodic: -P <far table from periodicn.py> -A <cube edge>. The cell is a
  forest of n1 x n2 x n3 root cubes; 6pi x 4pi x 2pi is 3 x 2 x 1 with
  A = 2pi. The near region is tree-handled (cube offsets <= 1, with image
  shifts); the far region is one operator per root offset, plus the exact
  (2pi/3V) sum q|x-y|^2 term.
  -g prints max/min |coefficient| per level and order, and counts
  non-finite and subnormal values.
  Files:
    tables.py     translation and rotation tables (mpmath)
    periodicn.py  far tables (split Ewald, normalised)
    periodic.py   point-charge Ewald reference and the old tables
    harmn.py, fmmn.py  numpy normalised reference
    gate3.py      CUDA vs numpy
    gateP.py      periodic vs Ewald
    deep.sh       20-level test
    bench2.sh     benchmark
    kahan.sh      compensated-summation test
  Results: DERIV.md section 8; out/B_tests.txt; out/rot_highprec_check.txt.

Lattice near field (fmm3.cu -L <neartab.py table>, -O 8 order-8 core)
  Uniform B^3 leaf blocks of cell-centred nodes; the near field is a
  precomputed per-offset linear map. FMM_NEAR=gemm (default) | fft.
  specref2.py gives the reference fields (regularised particles, and the true
  spectral velocity). lat1.sh / lat2.sh run the sweeps; results are in
  DERIV.md section 10 and out/lat*.out.

Lattice speed-ups (fmm3.cu -F, -G; derivations in DERIV.md section 11)
  -F <leaftab.py -B 8 -p P table>  P2M and L2P (with the curl) as two fixed
       real matrices applied by DGEMM to all leaves (needs -L).
  -G <m2ltab.py -p P table>  M2L as 26 GEMMs per level on shifted views of a
       padded, child-stacked copy of M (needs a uniform tree; with -G it is
       the default, FMM_M2L=1 forces the rotation kernel).
  Near field (-L, GEMM mode, always on): 6 of 9 products, padded leaf grid,
       no per-offset gather/combine.
  Tables: mpmath 40 digits, each entry rounded once (leaftab: 2-20 s,
  m2ltab: 1-20 s for p 8-20).
  Gates (out/opt_*.txt): velocity vs the previous code (fmm3_pinned.cu.bak)
  <= 9.7e-15 max relative at 1e8 p 20 (<= 4.8e-15 at 1.26e7); identical trees
  and U/V/W/X lists; errors vs the exact regularised velocity unchanged (192
  grid p 12: 6.073e-6, p 20: 4.869e-8); default path bitwise identical
  (cube, blob); gateP.py identical; -G on uniform non-lattice trees <= 1e-17.
  Kernel time (nsys), tube lattice, periodic 3x2x1, B 8, Kc 1:
                       GH200 (gh)                     A100 PCIe (hal GPU 3)
    N       p    before      after      ns/p          before    after    ns/p
    1.26e7  12   478 ms      79 ms      38.0 -> 6.2    887      247     70.5 -> 19.6
    1.26e7  16   697        122         55.4 -> 9.7   1533      398    121.8 -> 31.6
    1.26e7  20  1063        207         84.5 -> 16.5  2719      692    216.1 -> 55.0
    1.0e8   12  3771        561         37.5 -> 5.6   7082     1864     70.4 -> 18.5
    1.0e8   16  5524        889         54.9 -> 8.8  12226     2977    121.5 -> 29.6
    1.0e8   20  8435       1482         83.8 -> 14.7 21754     5134    216.1 -> 51.0
  GH200 1e8 split (ms, before -> after): P2M 667 -> 6.4 (p 12), 1671 -> 11.8
  (p 20); L2P 401 -> 11.6, 1058 -> 27; M2L 2077 -> 164, 5031 -> 1010; near
  562 -> 314 (6 of 9 alone: 468).
